7592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15e93809-1fc8-5035-b5b6-cb4d0ca11d45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194554 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194554 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "263c53e8-2f06-5be5-9f6a-884b62308d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198053 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198053 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/d8/d8be5d5bd5fa2cd8b618087109678a4f891b08f3788d4946d07bc1b5e4b39a3a.png)\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at ![](images/a2/a2dbcf34ea50e451efc96ad2f9a7422a7ae507ca8f74088569110abb25293954.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea202ce3-07d4-5324-9ee4-f5617b430772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198053 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/17/17588b058b1e1fe934e06679836234f0ef35fe4484a558fcc3ec3b2aafbd2620.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/61/613ba027e9564812a8e050efd69fd98fff3f614f7d2ec69eeb787c628ccabd12.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/87/87579b882ca6d8c92d4a7adfed575873fd07c36ab20251722c2cc8fc0f0328e8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/9f/9fc2431aa874c68b23b6640da987b2f3bc37e2261936f7e1656b04bc3fc0030d.png)\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at ![](images/b8/b83c8f9cb4130103d63821f0d0de50ec0628138dcc8faaf16e8cf9fc27f1a994.png) is\n\n![](images/08/0851481c21c553b6e0e06a795815b1845e96e0a14f805e440529d0f4d6f85323.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198054 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198054 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ defined as\n\n![](images/9f/9fb4fd5dfb788d65cc7ea3fd67bce06097c24e03454c3bd0c2971a4c24789f98.png)\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by \n\n![](images/e5/e5100618308bb9c2d07f0a022c18da152d88f30a3c7f3e0e1e173e9d743ff278.png)\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea76d21f-eff8-5634-975f-2b5bd24caf54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198054 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/f1/f1c18d26fb7b6f1969bcda14d71ac18429466b4be8847097f227cdc537fdc8d9.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/4b/4b752e33d4ca3154590a5dc16ec3addd1d45a9618c92283903ff33d3818b8266.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/d1/d10e552c42ff8fe0df517b6297582bd5eeefdf29896a51005c79bd399a363c13.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/8c/8c2cef2ba5322cc545860caa4d2f503f5f48a3374cce7c0f1e910a56f0357a64.png)\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198055 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198055 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/d1/d18d3d4b6ce4dc901608a0c8b91b245aa8922ad950d7b664b4de8320aa3c0818.png)\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at ![](images/62/62277cb0f04f6106a3039c916f28fbdf561bfdba6e08d576f1ed906b973ad90d.png) is given by\n\n![](images/d2/d2fcbb3375b9efa65559ba261581cf349dc801b3d84be5b465c36a7f4027c556.png)\n\nfind the missing values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db0be438-94fe-52de-9daf-60d1727ce7b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198055 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/e7/e79145a13ada389ac337a69f8c48952f53b70d5caf48d950b305275ed8773b44.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/a4/a4de8e3eca77241030facac81ea44493400e00e34d1c7dde9ee9b25eab8441b0.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/5e/5ee75b93739a5018486c6b65a5e5c996f95b7f8ea6fdf6bd68b5727742e770fa.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})=3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/a8/a8fd9120b29337c656c9b715bfa38dbecc7755e50877fad9719de54ed248a8ed.png)\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at ![](images/44/445be7536a39ee9c8d90f4cee571bf46acb5c995a9b53547f1aa582bd351592a.png) we have\n\n![](images/44/44faf9caacb8100249410ce233429c030fc52656be35c31d22f423397288da03.png)\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/58/5824c66708813f59e2920287d35500ea68356ead4a4c3673d8807a0b81ca1abe.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198056 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198056 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ defined as\n\n![](images/27/27a38861d0d2b0a8be98704202927c7a0dc0c638d11f25733b5a1e24faa18de2.png)\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at ![](images/fa/fafc23364eb261c2d42baebfee5aec21491567c7c8d7cdbeb5c1f5c6c9d30ae8.png) is given by\n\n![](images/b8/b86418e6cd8fdf247d775882927c1a9d7a6e0cd392f8bd79b93e6b58f267fef7.png)\n\nwhat are the missing values?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3540571-9159-50b4-a46c-4cf28a454602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198056 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/eb/eb248ccd80e8a10edab9d44c4cb11841aad35f6d35a778ff828607a312fcad35.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/47/47d512d278eaa912a112e96406e44d8b9a464984487e0ab9ff253a233441c2e1.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/4b/4bb0fb226d008d5efe2e7a04a444a2313b2acf80f71589a2aa0a17e40246c03b.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})={x}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/9e/9eb84e24979469e9b1618f0a20f030963741a513292699ed4c4d342cd9d8e00d.png)\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at ![](images/22/22f4d34b8ec830740b285e1c31954b1e382264c4f366d3ea426ecadd3fd5f5bb.png) we have\n\n![](images/65/651b14a10334bdfc073b86359ce306c1b4ba25b9390f345b609978e34cc08cad.png)\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/a0/a00401d8a9246532f363238c37646a6640a904db7f53d165d4f29c07e0a81a01.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198411 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198